\n     \\]\n\n     By construction each \\(U_a\\) is sunny, contains the point \\((a,1)\\), and contains **no other** point of \\(S_n\\).  \n     Choose the \\(k\\) numbers \\(m_a\\) pairwise distinct; then the lines \\(\\{U_a\\}_{a\\in A}\\) are distinct.\n\n   * **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n     For every integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) and \\(c\\notin A\\) (there are exactly \\(n-k\\) such integers) take the vertical line  \n\n     \\[\n     V_c:\\; x=c .\n     \\]\n\n     A vertical line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.\n\n   * **Verification that all points are covered.**  \n     Let \\((x,y)\\in S_n\\).  \n     - If \\(x\\in A\\) then \\((x,y)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(U_x\\) (because \\(U_x\\) passes through \\((x,1)\\) and, being a straight line, also passes through every other point with the same abscissa \\(x\\)).  \n     - If \\(x\\notin A\\) then \\(x=c\\) for some \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) and \\(c\\notin A\\); consequently \\((x,y)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_c\\).  \n\n     Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n   * We have produced exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{U_a\\}_{a\\in A}\\) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny vertical lines \\(\\{V_c\\}\\), altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions.\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); together with the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) this shows that the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), and \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound\n\nIf a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, then obviously \\(k\\le n\\). Hence any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nA:=\\{\\,n-k+1,\\;n-k+2,\\dots ,n\\,\\},\n\\qquad |A|=k .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(a\\in A\\) the lattice point \\((a,1)\\) belongs to \\(S_n\\) because \\(a+1\\le n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Sunny lines\n\nFor a fixed \\(a\\in A\\) consider the finite set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal M_a:=\\Bigl\\{\\,\\frac{b'-1}{a'-a}\\;\\Big|\\;(a',b')\\in S_n,\\;a'\\neq a\\,\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach element of \\(\\mathcal M_a\\) is the slope of the unique line through \\((a,1)\\) and another point of \\(S_n\\).  \nThe slopes \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)) are also forbidden for a sunny line.\n\nBecause \\(\\mathcal M_a\\) is finite, we can choose a real number \\(m_a\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nm_a\\notin\\mathcal M_a\\cup\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nU_a:\\; y-1=m_a\\,(x-a).\n\\]\n\n* \\(U_a\\) passes through \\((a,1)\\) by definition.  \n* Since \\(m_a\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), the line is sunny.  \n* Because \\(m_a\\notin\\mathcal M_a\\), the line does **not** contain any other point of \\(S_n\\).  \n\nChoose the numbers \\(\\{m_a\\}_{a\\in A}\\) pairwise distinct; then the lines \\(\\{U_a\\}_{a\\in A}\\) are distinct.  \nThus we have constructed exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Non‑sunny lines\n\nFor every integer  \n\n\\[\nc\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus A\n\\]\n\ndefine the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_c:\\; x=c .\n\\]\n\nA vertical line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nThere are exactly \\(n-k\\) such integers \\(c\\), so we obtain \\(n-k\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Coverage of all points of \\(S_n\\)\n\nTake any \\((x,y)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(x\\in A\\):* then \\((x,y)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(U_x\\) because \\(U_x\\) contains every point whose abscissa equals \\(x\\) (it is a straight line through \\((x,1)\\)).\n\n*If \\(x\\notin A\\):* then \\(x=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) and \\(c\\notin A\\); consequently \\((x,y)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_c\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Counting the lines\n\nWe have produced  \n\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{U_a\\}_{a\\in A}\\);\n* \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny vertical lines \\(\\{V_c\\}\\).\n\nAll these lines are distinct, their total number is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nTogether with the coverage proved in §2.3, the two conditions of the problem are satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* Necessarily \\(k\\le n\\).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required properties exists (the construction of §2).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**End of solution.**\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 sma